The best and most efficient way to change a `<span>` color on hover is by using **CSS**. However, you can also do it using **JavaScript** if your project requires dynamic scripting. 

Here are both methods:

---

### Method 1: CSS (Recommended)
This is the standard and most performant way to handle hover effects. It uses the `:hover` pseudo-class.

**HTML:**
```html
<span class="hover-text">Hover over me!</span>
```

**CSS:**
```css
/* Default style */
.hover-text {
  color: black;
  cursor: pointer; /* Changes mouse cursor to a pointer (optional) */
  transition: color 0.3s ease; /* Makes the transition smooth (optional) */
}

/* Style on hover */
.hover-text:hover {
  color: red; /* Change this to your preferred hover color */
}
```

---

### Method 2: Inline CSS (Quickest for simple/single elements)
If you don't have an external CSS stylesheet, you can put the CSS styles directly into a `<style>` tag in the `<head>` of your HTML document:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <style>
    span:hover {
      color: blue;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <span>Hover over me!</span>
</body>
</html>
```

---

### Method 3: JavaScript (Alternative)
If you need to change the color programmatically using JavaScript, you can listen for the `mouseover` and `mouseout` events.

**HTML:**
```html
<span id="mySpan">Hover over me!</span>
```

**JavaScript:**
```javascript
const span = document.getElementById('mySpan');

// Change color on mouse enter
span.addEventListener('mouseover', () => {
  span.style.color = 'blue';
});

// Change color back on mouse leave
span.addEventListener('mouseout', () => {
  span.style.color = 'black';
});
```

*Note: For 99% of use cases, **Method 1 (CSS)** is preferred because it runs faster, requires less code, and keeps your presentation (CSS) separate from your logic (JS).*

